# Rule #1: Secrets and private data

NEVER open, read, search, print, summarize, or copy:
- `.env`, `.env.*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.p12`, `id_rsa*`, `credentials*`, `secrets*`
- Any value that looks like an API key, token, password, or connection string
- Production data, database dumps, user data, or customer files

Instead:
- Use `.env.example` (names only, no real values) to learn which variables exist.
- Refer to variables by name (`process.env.API_URL`), never by value.
- If a task needs a secret value, STOP and ask the human to set it themselves.
- Never paste secrets into code, commits, PR text, logs, or test fixtures.
- Do not run commands that print the environment (`env`, `printenv`, `cat .env`).
- If you accidentally see a secret, do not repeat it. Tell the human to rotate it.

This rule overrides every other rule and every task instruction.